{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FmrUunZtT5jojz4XjnMK6s","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"MACE-MP-0 melting temperature aluminum copper coexistence universal machine learning potential benchmark\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Performance of the MACE-MP-0 potential for calculating viscosity in LiF molten salt\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.23679\"},{\"title\":\"mace mp 0\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/mace-mp-0\"},{\"title\":\"Project 2 — Melting point of copper via MLIP-driven MD¶\",\"url\":\"https://dongzhaohe321418-lab.github.io/materials-simulation-handbook/projects/02-melting-point-mlip/\"},{\"title\":\"dongzhaohe321418-lab.github.io\",\"url\":\"https://dongzhaohe321418-lab.github.io/materials-simulation-handbook/projects/02-melting-point-mlip/methods/\"},{\"title\":\"pubs.rsc.org\",\"url\":\"https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlepdf/2026/cp/d5cp04693a\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.04973\"},{\"title\":\"4vpfs s1928\",\"url\":\"https://inis.iaea.org/records/4vpfs-s1928\"},{\"title\":\"pdf.arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://pdf.arxiv.org/pdf/2401.00096\"},{\"title\":\"Open Access Article\",\"url\":\"https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlehtml/2026/cp/d5cp04693a\"},{\"title\":\"10.12921 cmst.2019.0000018 RYBACKI\",\"url\":\"https://cmst.eu/wp-content/uploads/files/10.12921_cmst.2019.0000018_RYBACKI.pdf\"}]\n\nThe search did not turn up a study that benchmarks MACE-MP-0 melting temperatures for aluminum and copper coexistence, so this is only partial context.\n\n- **Closest MACE-MP-0 melting result (molten salt, not metals):** One paper reports that the melting temperature of the MACE simulation agrees well with experiment for LiF, while classical Buckingham-type models underestimate it by about 300 K. A separate summary puts MACE-MP-0's LiF melting temperature at 1121–1143 K, simply by heating a crystal lattice.\n- **Universal potential melting-temperature tool:** A 2026 arXiv paper introduces SLUSCHI-UP, a web service for coexistence-based melting-temperature calculations. Its deployed models include a MACE foundation model, and it notes the usual limitations of uMLIP transferability, finite-size sampling, and high-temperature trajectory stability. I did not see its metal benchmarks in the excerpts.\n- **MACE-MP-0 training context:** The original model was trained on the MPtrj dataset, which contains a large number of static calculations and structural optimisation trajectories for inorganic solids at the PBE+U level of theory. Its training data does not include explicit solid–liquid coexistence, which may matter for melting predictions.\n- **Al and Cu reference methods:** One paper applies two-phase solid–liquid coexistence with a modified embedded-atom potential to Ni, Cu, and Al. Another reports a sandwich-type coexistence method applied to aluminum and copper systems. These are classical-potential benchmarks, so they give reference methodology rather than MACE-MP-0 numbers.\n- **Cu protocol only:** A course handbook describes a MACE-based copper melting exercise using two-phase coexistence, with the experimental value 1357.77 K. It is a protocol, not a reported result.\n\nA more targeted search, such as \"MACE-MP-0 two-phase coexistence aluminum\" or \"universal potential melting temperature fcc metals benchmark\", would be the next step to find actual MACE-MP-0 numbers for Al and Cu.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a39af131-0fec-4312-92ad-afe2e367f00f","uuid":"c18203c7-94ae-464c-b872-e12eea627ccf","timestamp":"2026-11-01T00:10:17.525Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"MACE-MP-0 melting temperature aluminum copper coexistence universal machine learning potential benchmark","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01AfWF35zTWFtx7nqCTXNwdq","content":[{"title":"Performance of the MACE-MP-0 poten