iginal model was trained on the MPtrj dataset, which contains a large number of static calculations and structural optimisation trajectories for inorganic solids at the PBE+U level of theory. Its training data does not include explicit solid–liquid coexistence, which may matter for melting predictions.\n- **Al and Cu reference methods:** One paper applies two-phase solid–liquid coexistence with a modified embedded-atom potential to Ni, Cu, and Al. Another reports a sandwich-type coexistence method applied to aluminum and copper systems. These are classical-potential benchmarks, so they give reference methodology rather than MACE-MP-0 numbers.\n- **Cu protocol only:** A course handbook describes a MACE-based copper melting exercise using two-phase coexistence, with the experimental value 1357.77 K. It is a protocol, not a reported result.\n\nA more targeted search, such as \"MACE-MP-0 two-phase coexistence aluminum\" or \"universal potential melting temperature fcc metals benchmark\", would be the next step to find actual MACE-MP-0 numbers for Al and Cu."],"durationSeconds":6.225836439999984,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01FmrUunZtT5jojz4XjnMK6s","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
